Yes—several open-source and commercial tools replicate data across databases, though SymmetricDS remains the strongest open-source fit if you specifically require multi-master (active-active) replication between both MySQL and PostgreSQL.
Most modern replication tools rely on log-based Change Data Capture (CDC) (reading binary logs or WAL files) rather than database triggers. While log-based tools place less overhead on your database, very few support out-of-the-box bidirectional conflict resolution across mixed database engines without custom engineering. [1] (https://www.info2soft.com/blogs/symmetricds-alternative.html), [2] (https://wiki.postgresql.org/wiki/Replication,_Clustering,_and_Connection_Pooling), [3] (https://severalnines.com/blog/overview-postgresql-mysql-cross-replication/)

Comparison of SymmetricDS Alternatives


Tool	License / Model	Best Use Case	Multi-Master & Conflict Resolution	MySQL + Postgres Cross-Sync
SymmetricDS	Open Source (GPL) / Pro	Multi-region, multi-master sync across heterogeneous DBs	Yes — built-in (NEWER_WINS, FALLBACK, etc.)	Yes — full bidirectional sync
Debezium	Open Source (Apache 2.0)	Streaming DB changes into Kafka/event pipelines	No — requires custom consumers & conflict logic	Partial — captures changes; needs JDBC sink connectors
Bucardo	Open Source (BSD)	Multi-master replication strictly for PostgreSQL	Yes — built-in conflict handlers	No — PostgreSQL targets only (can read from MySQL)
pgEdge / Spock	Open Source (PostgreSQL)	High-speed multi-master PostgreSQL clusters	Yes — built-in conflict resolution & delta-apply	No — PostgreSQL only
DBConvert Streams	Commercial (Self-Hosted)	Continuous log-based CDC between MySQL & Postgres	Limited — primarily unidirectional / migration-focused	Yes — native MySQL <-> PostgreSQL
Oracle GoldenGate / Qlik Replicate	Enterprise Commercial	High-volume, mission-critical enterprise replication	Yes — advanced conflict detection & resolution	Yes — full heterogeneous support


Key Architectural Trade-Offs to Consider

• If you must mix MySQL and PostgreSQL in Active-Active mode: Stick with SymmetricDS (or an enterprise tool like Qlik Replicate / GoldenGate). Trigger-based capture works on almost any managed DBaaS without needing superuser access to binary logs or WAL streams, which many cloud DBaaS providers restrict. [1] (https://severalnines.com/blog/overview-postgresql-mysql-cross-replication/), [2] (https://www.info2soft.com/blogs/symmetricds-alternative.html)
• If you can standardize on PostgreSQL only: pgEdge (Spock) or Bucardo are purpose-built for multi-master PostgreSQL. Spock uses logical replication (WAL) for much lower latency and overhead than triggers, though managed DBaaS providers (like DigitalOcean or AWS RDS) often restrict custom C extensions like Spock. [1] (https://www.percona.com/blog/multi-master-replication-solutions-for-postgresql/), [2] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YE3pF0YAImE&t=499)
• If you can switch to Event-Driven Architecture: Debezium (paired with Kafka or Redpanda) is the industry standard for log-based CDC, but it is a streaming tool rather than a turnkey multi-master database replicator—you would have to write your own conflict-resolution and loop-prevention code. [1] (https://opensource.com/article/21/8/database-replication-open-source), [2] (https://www.domo.com/learn/article/data-sync-platforms)
• If you want zero replication middleware: Consider migrating to a Distributed SQL database (such as CockroachDB or YugabyteDB). These databases natively handle multi-region active-active writes, consensus, and conflict resolution at the storage layer without external sync engines. [1] (https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-iygJUpcgE0&t=514)
If you'd like to narrow down whether another tool fits better, let me know:
• Which DBaaS provider you are using (e.g., DigitalOcean, Linode, AWS RDS, Vultr) — as managed database permissions often dictate whether log-based CDC is even allowed
• Whether mixing MySQL and PostgreSQL is a strict legacy requirement or if you can standardize on one engine
